tree-sitter-typescript 0.23.2 vendor

Base source: https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-typescript/tree/v0.23.2
Base commit: f975a621f4e7f532fe322e13c4f79495e0a7b2e7
License: MIT (see LICENSE)

Local grammar patch provenance:
https://github.com/jhanssen/tree-sitter-typescript/commit/1b3e602cd4021dd390ecdeb72ae8e37bbca402e8

Only the import-in-type-position portion of that commit is applied in
common/define-grammar.js:
- remove the special import member/call aliases from `type`;
- add those aliases to `primary_type`, making them reachable through
  `index_type_query` and other type operators;
- permit the aliased import member expression as a `generic_type` name.

The unrelated accessor-modifier changes from 1b3e602 are intentionally not
included.

The JSX grammar is also patched so JSX element, namespace, and attribute
names accept JavaScript/TypeScript reserved words as IdentifierName values
without relaxing ordinary identifier parsing. The TSX-only conflict entries
in common/define-grammar.js keep type-parameter and type-member ambiguity
explicit and leave the TypeScript dialect branch unchanged.

TypeScript 4.7 variance annotations on type parameters are supported
locally in common/define-grammar.js: `type_parameter` accepts an optional
`in`, `out`, or combined `in out` modifier before the parameter name.
Repeated or reversed modifiers (`out in`, `out out`) remain parse errors so
that fail-closed source facts keep flagging malformed input. Upstream
0.23.2 and master do not support these annotations (openho/hoonarqube#94).

The shared export grammar accepts TypeScript's type-only wildcard re-exports,
`export type * from "module"` and `export type * as Name from "module"`, as
used by Got. Both wildcard forms require a `from` clause; named type exports
retain their existing optional `from` clause.

The generated TypeScript and TSX parser sources, node-type tables, and
matching tree-sitter support headers were regenerated from this narrowed
grammar with tree-sitter CLI 0.25.10 using ABI 14, with
tree-sitter-javascript 0.23.1 as the grammar base.

The shared external scanner permits a line break before '<' to separate
type members, including adjacent generic call signatures (Redux's
StoreCreator). As for '(' and '[', the scanner checks whether a binary
operator is valid so expression continuations keep their original tree.
This scanner-only change applies to both dialects without regenerating
the parsers.

Property signatures accept `abstract` as a `property_identifier`, including
Pinia's optional `abstract?: boolean` in an interface and a nested object
type. This addresses the property signature case of upstream
tree-sitter-typescript #377. Both parser dialects use the same rule; the
class modifier rules are unchanged. The generated files use the CLI,
ABI, and JavaScript grammar version documented above.
